			<div class="content" id="post-content-3975644">
									

<p>Two objects falling through a near-vacuum from the same starting position towards the surface of a planet have a decent chance of eventually coming into contact with one another. Especially if one of those things is biological and would tend to, shall we say, flatten out a bit on impact.</p>
<p>You are correct that it is completely infeasible that fossil records would be able to definitely identify a human. However, it would potentially be sufficient to suggest that such a fossil was formed by a multi-cellular organism, at a point in time in which no multi-cellular life is suspected to exist on the planet.</p>

